WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.123 --> 00:00:01.743
- Here are a few things you should

00:00:01.743 --> 00:00:04.065
probably be doing with your shirts.

00:00:04.065 --> 00:00:05.738
When wearing a white dress shirt,

00:00:05.738 --> 00:00:09.938
undershirts of the same
color usually show through.

00:00:09.938 --> 00:00:12.650
A grey undershirt is
typically less visible,

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:16.272
as it more closely matches
the value of skintone.

00:00:16.272 --> 00:00:18.501
An easy way to get a good sleeve roll

00:00:18.501 --> 00:00:20.637
is to pull the cuff up like so,

00:00:20.637 --> 00:00:23.098
and fold the sleeve over it here.

00:00:23.098 --> 00:00:24.768
Jack Kennedy did it this way,

00:00:24.768 --> 00:00:26.906
so it's probably good enough for you.

00:00:26.906 --> 00:00:29.182
Simply shoving your shirt into your pants

00:00:29.182 --> 00:00:31.411
can cause severe muffin-topping.

00:00:31.411 --> 00:00:33.501
To get a clean tuck, grab
the bottom of the shirt

00:00:33.501 --> 00:00:36.055
and fold the fabric
back at the side seams.

00:00:36.055 --> 00:00:38.098
This hides extra fabric and is great

00:00:38.098 --> 00:00:40.327
for shirts that are particularly wide.

00:00:40.327 --> 00:00:42.556
Or you could just buy
shirts that actually fit.

00:00:42.556 --> 00:00:44.459
And while we're on the subject of tucking,

00:00:44.459 --> 00:00:46.411
shirts with the longer, curved tails

00:00:46.411 --> 00:00:48.174
are designed to be tucked.

00:00:48.174 --> 00:00:49.884
Shirts with flat bottoms are better suited

00:00:49.884 --> 00:00:51.369
for leaving untucked.

00:00:51.369 --> 00:00:52.949
And those little sleeves on your collar

00:00:52.949 --> 00:00:54.760
are for collar stays.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:59.760
Use them to achieve a
more clean, formal look.

00:00:59.889 --> 00:01:01.398
Want to update an old shirt?

00:01:01.398 --> 00:01:03.720
You can turn a shirt
collar into a band collar.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:06.248
Simply cut off the collar here.

00:01:06.248 --> 00:01:07.637
You can also pull the stitching,

00:01:07.637 --> 00:01:09.386
remove the collar, and resew it,

00:01:09.386 --> 00:01:11.614
but you're probably gonna [bleep] that up.

00:01:11.614 --> 00:01:14.495
(African percussion music)

